# Recruitzaa — Backend Architecture Plan (v3 — FE-Aligned)

> **Authored by**: Senior Backend Architect (10 YOE)
> **Supersedes**: BE_PLAN_2
> **Stack**: Python FastAPI · Microservices · Kafka · MongoDB + PostgreSQL + Redis
> **Auth**: Firebase Admin SDK + RBAC + Multi-Role Session System
> **Roles (v3)**: CANDIDATE · EMPLOYER · EXPERT · EMPLOYEE · SUPER_ADMIN (5 roles total)
> **Routing**: FE uses HashRouter — all API calls always to the same origin

---

## What Changed from v2 → v3 (and Why)

### Change 1: 5 Roles instead of 3

v2 said: CANDIDATE / EMPLOYER / SUPER_ADMIN
v3 says: CANDIDATE / EMPLOYER / EXPERT / EMPLOYEE / SUPER_ADMIN

WHY: web/src/types/auth.types.ts line 1 defines:

  export type UserRole = 'CANDIDATE' | 'EMPLOYER' | 'EXPERT' | 'EMPLOYEE' | 'SUPER_ADMIN';

The FE has full dedicated dashboards and route guards for all 5 roles.
The BE users table schema and require_role() dependency must reflect this.

---

### Change 2: Multi-Role Session System (The Biggest Change)

v2 said: Each user has exactly ONE role stored in PostgreSQL.
v3 says: Each user has a PRIMARY role but may have MULTIPLE available roles.
         They choose the active role at login via the Enterprise Launchpad.

WHY: web/src/types/auth.types.ts defines the AppUser interface:

  export interface AppUser {
    id: string;
    email: string;
    role: UserRole;              // backward-compat primary role
    availableRoles?: UserRole[]; // all roles this user can access
    activeRole?: UserRole;       // what they chose in this session
    ...
  }

The FE LaunchpadPage.tsx reads appUser.availableRoles and shows a portal card
for each role. RoleGuard.tsx line 85 checks:
  if (!appUser || !allowedRoles.includes(appUser.activeRole || appUser.role)) {
    return <Navigate to="/unauthorized" replace />;
  }

Backend implications:
- PostgreSQL users table gains available_roles: text[] column
- GET /auth/me response must include available_roles[] alongside role
- Redis session cache stores { id, email, role, available_roles[], ... }

---

### Change 3: LinkedIn OIDC Provider

v2 said: Google + GitHub social login.
v3 says: Google + GitHub + LinkedIn (OIDC) social login.

WHY: web/src/config/firebase.ts already imports OAuthProvider.
     web/src/services/auth.service.ts already exports signInWithLinkedIn.

NO BACKEND CODE CHANGE NEEDED. Firebase handles OIDC. The BE receives a
standard Firebase ID token regardless of sign-in method.

---

### Change 4: FE Allowlist — BE Must Not Rely on It for Security

v2 said: Nothing about allowlists.
v3 says: The FE has web/src/config/allowlist.txt granting certain emails all 5
         roles. This is a FRONTEND-ONLY convenience for demo/staging.
         It is NOT a security boundary.

WHY THIS MATTERS: An attacker can bypass the FE allowlist by forging a token.
The BE must validate roles from its own PostgreSQL users.available_roles column.
Replicate the allowlist in BE via ADMIN_EMAILS env var for staging only.

